# Reader review and proposed navigation

Reviewed the 2,503-line, approximately 21,300-word walkthrough at SHA256 `78e957ce20c431633b7ba30b75eea1779d9df450eaed02bfea3a20883516bfed`. This is a reader-oriented review, separate from the completed evidence audit. No active document, source, build or GPU state was changed.

The guide contains the right material: what changed, why arithmetic limits the choices, what failed, and what each measurement actually covers. Its main difficulty is that a tutorial gradually becomes a chronological experiment log. Readers can lose the thread while moving among network shapes, build numbers, kernel variants and historical measurements. Keep that detail, but provide a short thematic route before it. A 36-item flat table of contents would mostly repeat the long headings.

## Proposed reader's map fragment

The following fragment could replace the existing “If you want to understand…” table. Its links are intended for insertion into the walkthrough itself.

> Read sections 1–3 for the computation and numerical rules, then choose a route below. Later sections retain the experiment history, including unsuccessful attempts. Build tags such as v24 and v35 identify compiled releases; C512 means a 512-channel stage, while block 30 names one particular network entry. The opening status table identifies what is currently integrated.

| Read to understand… | Route through the guide |
|---|---|
| The network and why ordinary replacements change its result | [The computation](#1-start-with-the-actual-computation), [rounding and accumulation](#2-numerical-details-are-part-of-the-kernel-interface), [training versus deployment](#3-keep-training-straightforward-and-deployment-specialized) |
| The main tricks that remove inference work | [Cache weights and keep packed activations](#4-stop-repeatedly-converting-weights-and-activations); sections 5–8 show progressively larger fusions; [direct layout conversion](#28-replace-indexed-trunk-conversions-with-direct-byte-permutations) removes endpoint indexing |
| Why a kernel is slow and how we decide what to change | [NCU and executable assembly](#10-use-ncu-and-sass-to-choose-the-next-change), [register lifetimes](#16-shorten-register-lifetimes-without-changing-arithmetic), [whole-execution attribution](#30-profile-the-complete-execution-before-choosing-the-next-kernel) |
| How timings, correctness and the native comparison are established | [CUDA Graph measurement](#9-make-cuda-graph-timing-representative), [the original-kernel reference](#12-build-a-trustworthy-original-kernel-reference), [the complete native range and its limits](#25-complete-the-native-trunk-comparison-across-the-requested-range) |
| How every input resolution gets a compiled choice | [Continuous tuning and endpoint clamping](#11-tune-continuous-resolutions-then-compile-the-decisions), [actual cache operands and C++ dispatch](#27-put-measured-cache-choices-behind-c-dispatch) |
| Which newer ideas are still being evaluated | [Large ordered partitions](#32-give-large-gemm-tiles-enough-independent-work), [C512 fusion](#33-fuse-c512-qkv-projection-attention-and-publication), [its prefetch crossover](#34-prefetch-the-next-c512-weight-fragment-then-measure-the-crossover), [vector reduction](#35-reduce-eight-half-values-per-thread-without-changing-the-sum) |
| What failed, and what the current build actually enables | [A failed prefetch](#21-a-prefetch-experiment-that-did-not-earn-deployment), [cache hints](#29-retained-cache-hints-can-still-make-a-kernel-slower), [the asynchronous ring](#31-an-asynchronous-shared-memory-ring-that-worked-but-lost); finish with [current catalogue and validation state](#36-keep-public-choices-and-profiling-evidence-unambiguous) |

The first route establishes the constraints. The next two explain most of the optimization decisions. The measurement and tuning routes explain why an isolated improvement cannot immediately become a production policy. The final two routes are the detailed research history and current integration status.

## Terms to explain before asking the reader to interpret them

The existing C/M/K/MMA/warp/CTA/SM paragraph is useful. Add **N**, then define the following at first use; a second large glossary at the top is unnecessary.

| Term and first difficult use | Short explanation to insert |
|---|---|
| GEMM, opening table and §4 | A general matrix multiplication. For an output with M rows and N columns, K is the dimension summed over. |
| FFN and QKV, opening table | FFN is the feed-forward subnetwork; Q, K and V are attention's query, key and value projections. Attention's key K and a GEMM's reduction dimension K are different uses of the same letter. |
| E4M3, §2 | The FP8 representation used here: four exponent bits and three fraction bits, plus a sign bit. “Packed” stores those encodings as bytes. |
| NCU, SASS and PTX, §2/§4 | NCU is NVIDIA Nsight Compute, the kernel profiler. PTX is the intermediate GPU instruction representation; SASS is the native instruction sequence that the GPU actually executes. |
| ATen, §3; KDA, §10 | ATen is PyTorch's tensor-operator library. KDA refers to NVIDIA's Kernel Design Agents tooling/skills used for the optimization workflow. |
| BHWC, first explicit use in §28 | Tensor dimensions in batch, height, width, channel order. Introduce this before the first four-number tensor shape in §4. |
| Cubin and NGX, §1/§12 | A cubin contains compiled CUDA device code. NGX is NVIDIA's host integration/runtime layer; launching extracted kernels does not exercise that host layer. |
| “Raw” and “publication,” §2/§4 | Raw values are the Half results before the specified outgoing publication. Publication applies the required rounding/encoding at a network boundary; it need not write a separate tensor. |
| “Resident,” §4 onward | Inputs, weights and buffers are already on the GPU. The local timed work excludes loading the model and preparing those tensors unless explicitly included. |
| “Canonical” and weight “cache,” §7/§27 | Canonical means the retained ordinary weight layout. The prepared cache is an additional software tensor layout, not the GPU's hardware L2 cache. |
| Long-scoreboard stall, §4 | A warp is waiting for a result of an outstanding memory operation before it can use that value. A high count locates a dependency; it does not alone prove saturated memory bandwidth. |
| Occupancy and register spill, §5 onward | Occupancy measures resident warps relative to capacity. A spill moves register-held values into local memory. Both affect performance, but neither alone predicts elapsed time. |
| Anchor, dispatch key and guard, §11 | An anchor is a measured workload size with a selected configuration. A dispatch key contains the properties that must match before transferring that choice. A guard anchor explicitly retains the existing path where a new candidate failed. |

FMA and epilogue already receive useful plain-language explanations in their own sections; no additional top-level expansion is needed. Hardware instruction mnemonics such as `HFMA2`, `LDS.128` and `cp.async` can remain in the detailed sections, with a short action in prose at first use: “packed Half multiply-add,” “16-byte shared-memory load,” and “asynchronous copy into shared memory.”

## Numbers that need a small connecting sentence or key

These are comprehension issues, not newly identified inaccuracies.

1. **One 4K input produces several different matrix shapes.** The guide states padding correctly, but the reader later meets 1088×1920, 68×120 and M2160 without a compact cross-reference. A four-row key near the first performance table would connect them:

   | Meaning for a 3840×2160 visible image | Width × height, or flattened rows |
   |---|---:|
   | Padded full field | 3840 × 2176 |
   | First C32 field / native trunk endpoint | 1920 × 1088 |
   | C512 field | 120 × 68; M = 8160 |
   | Global C1024 field | 60 × 36; M = 2160 |

   These values were checked with the current pure-CPU `Geometry.from_valid`. Say that tensor shapes use **H×W** where the text above uses image **W×H**. This explains why “4K kernel” does not mean a 3840×2160 matrix.

2. **The ratio direction changes between legitimate comparison types.** Before the tables, give one small reading rule: “Baseline/candidate above 1 is faster; candidate/native above 1 is slower. A 1.10× speedup is about 9.1% lower latency, not 10% lower latency.” Existing table headings can stay unchanged. The opening status table puts both kinds close together, making this especially valuable.

3. **There are three independent kinds of acceptance.** A compact key would distinguish byte equality, the local promotion margin, and the 85% measured hardware-throughput target. Passing one does not imply another. The guide already explains this in several places; a single early definition of “gate” would prevent readers from mistaking the 3% policy margin for the user's native-speed requirement.

4. **Counts have different units.** Section 5 moves from 931 geometries to 3,626 dispatch keys, 11,996 candidate checks and 3,632 policy rows. The text accounts for the extra anchors, but one sentence defining geometry → key → candidate observation → emitted row would make these numbers usable without rereading. The same distinction later explains 258 C512 fields versus 166 M values. Keep their precise counts in the detailed evidence sections.

5. **Profile diagnosis and timing confirmation sometimes use different shapes.** Section 4 diagnoses M96, then quotes M640/M2160 timing gains. State the connection directly: the first profile motivated the design; the following timings test it on larger workloads. The existing different labels are accurate, but a newcomer may assume all numbers describe one experiment.

6. **Resource percentages and elapsed speed answer different questions.** Section 23 usefully notes that adapter/pool latency falls while DRAM percentage falls; section 35 gives a 1.46–1.50× reducer improvement but only about 4% for its enclosing contraction. Bring one such example forward as a reading rule: removing traffic can lower memory utilization while making the program faster. Do not make readers equate “closer to 85%” with “better overall latency.”

## Small editorial changes with a large payoff

- Keep the current status table, but make its version labels links to the corresponding detailed sections. A reader should not need to search for a build after seeing its result.
- Label §13 and §22 as historical results in their headings or first sentence. Their numerical contents are valid; their generic titles can look like the latest status when opened from search.
- At the beginning of a long experiment section, use one ordinary sentence stating the removed cost and the outcome. The later prefetch and reducer sections already do this well.
- Keep binary digests, exact sample counts and resource details close to the evidence links, after the mechanism and result. They are necessary for reproduction but interrupt a first explanation when introduced before the question they answer.
- Retain the failed experiments. They explain why superficially attractive changes were rejected and are among the most useful parts of the guide. Link to them from the matching successful technique rather than asking readers to infer that relationship from build numbers.

This navigation work can be applied now without claiming the task is complete. After the remaining scans and native comparisons finish, update the status table and final results section to the verified outcome. The user's requested final tricks document should retain the distinction between techniques that reached deployment, private candidates that passed local tests, and acceptance targets still unmet.
